### `\TLV` macro conventions (macros §7, verbatim guide example)

```
// wire(
//    /_top: Top-level scope.
//    /_name: A unique level of hierarchy for the instance.
//    $_in:   An input pipesignal in /_top scope, optionally with a bit range.
//    $_out:  An output signal in /_name with bit range (unless boolean).
// Connects an input signal to an output signal. Should be called within a pipestage.
\TLV wire(/_top, /_name, $_in, $_out)
   /_name
      $_out = /_top$_in;

\TLV
   |pipe
      @1
         $in_byte[7:0] = ...;
         m5+wire(|pipe, /connection1, $in_byte, $out[7:0])
         $stuff = /connection1$out
```

Prefix conventions: `$` pipesignal or expression, `|` pipeline path, `@` stage, `/` hierarchy
path, `#` elaboration-time constant, `$$_x` an output the macro assigns, none for text or
blocks. `/_top` is where the macro reads caller signals; `/_name` is the unique scope it
builds in so two calls do not collide. `/_top` is a path placeholder: the guide passes `|pipe`
to it. Header comments must state whether the macro is called inside a stage, inside a
pipeline, or outside both (macros §7). Real production shape: `warp-v/warp-v.tlv:3517`
`\TLV warpv_mul(/_top, /_name, $$_rslt, $_clk, $_reset, $_op_a, $_op_b, ...)`; the serv pilot
`serv_rf_ram_if_macro.tlv` takes only `/_top` and hardcodes names inside `|default @0`.

