### 3.2 Reset

(tutorial) `tutorial/default.tlv:14-15`, the required first line of any design:
```tlv
   // Connect SV inputs to TLV pipesignals.
   $reset = *reset;
```
(tutorial) `tutorial/makerchip_tutorial.tlv:67-70`, reset assigned inside the pipeline stage that consumes it, and `tutorial/makerchip_tutorial.tlv:183-185` (`|resp @1 $reset = *reset;`): each pipeline gets its own `$reset`.
```tlv
   |fetch
      // Next PC
      @0
         $reset = *reset;
```
(lib) `tlv_flow_lib/macro_tests/flop_fifo_v2_test.tlv:19-21`, a sub-scope pipeline pulls reset from the top with natural alignment:
```tlv
      |in
         @1
            $reset = /top<>0$reset;
```
(warp-v) `warp-v/warp-v.tlv:3779-3784`, reset stretched with a counter so it covers the pipeline depth:
```tlv
      @m5_stage_calc(@m5_NEXT_PC_STAGE<<1)
         $soft_reset = (m5_soft_reset) || *reset;
         $Cnt[7:0] <= $soft_reset   ? 8'b0 :       // reset
                      $Cnt == 8'hFF ? 8'hFF :      // max out to avoid wrapping
                                      $Cnt + 8'b1; // increment
         $reset = $soft_reset || $Cnt < m5_calc(m5_LD_RETURN_ALIGN + m5_MAX_REDIRECT_BUBBLES + 3);
```
Also: `tlv_flow_lib/grid_network.tlv:89-92` puts `$reset = *reset;` in its own `|reset @-1` pipeline; `tutorial/incrementer_example.tlv:20` and `makerchip_examples/DAC_ring_example.tlv:45` use `*reset` directly in expressions (library macros do this to avoid requiring a `$reset` in the caller's scope); `tutorial/webinar_certification.tlv:29` detects the end of reset with `(! $reset && >>1$reset)`.

### 3.3 Counters

(tutorial) `tutorial/tdm_example.tlv:23-24`, free-running wrap counter as a pipesignal, plus a derived pulse:
```tlv
         $cnt[1:0] = $reset ? 0 : >>1$cnt + 1;
         $valid = $cnt == 0 && ! $reset;
```
(tutorial) `tutorial/makerchip_tutorial.tlv:215-218`, cycle counter as state driving `*passed`:
```tlv
   $reset = *reset;
   $Cnt[15:0] <= $reset ? 16'b0 :
                               $Cnt + 16'b1;
   *passed = $Cnt > 16'd100;
```
(lib) `tlv_flow_lib/macro_tests/flop_fifo_v2_test.tlv:24-30`, conditional counters with `$RETAIN` and `'0`:
```tlv
            $Cnt[7:0] <= $reset       ? '0 :
                         $trans_valid ? $Cnt + 8'b1 :
                                        $RETAIN;
            // Count the number of times backpressure is applied to an available transaction since the last.
            $BackpressureCnt[7:0] <= $reset || $trans_valid   ? '0 :
                                     $avail && $blocked ? $BackpressureCnt + 8'b1 :
                                                          $RETAIN;
```
Also: `tutorial/div_example.tlv:81-83` (`@-1` free-running `$CycCnt`), `tutorial/mini_cpu_parameterized.tlv:408-414` (down-counting `$SquashCnt` loaded with macro constants), `makerchip_examples/claude_uart.tlv:61-64` (down counter producing a tick).

### 3.4 Valid / when-gated logic

(tutorial) `tutorial/pythagorean_example.tlv:10-25`, stimulus `$valid` then a `?$valid` block spanning three stages:
```tlv
   |calc
      @0
         $valid = & $rand_valid[1:0];  // Valid with 1/4 probability
                                       // (& over two random bits).

   // DUT (Design Under Test)
   |calc
      ?$valid
         // Pythagoras's Theorem
         @1
            $aa_sq[7:0] = $aa[3:0] ** 2;
            $bb_sq[7:0] = $bb[3:0] ** 2;
         @2
            $cc_sq[8:0] = $aa_sq + $bb_sq;
         @3
            $cc[4:0] = sqrt($cc_sq);
```
(tutorial) `tutorial/makerchip_tutorial.tlv:109-118`, `?$when` inside a stage with the stated rule:
```tlv
      @4
         // Condition on valid instruction if pipelined implementation.
         ?$instr_valid
            // Instruction fields:
            $opcode[3:0]  = $ir[15:12];
            $dest[2:0]    = $ir[11:9];
            $src1[2:0]    = $ir[8:6];
            $src2[2:0]    = $ir[2:0];
            $offset6[5:0] = $ir[5:0];
            $offset9[8:0] = $ir[8:0];
```
(tutorial) `tutorial/bp_pipe_example.tlv:41-45`, in flow-library pipelines the condition is `?$accepted`, not `?$valid`:
```tlv
   |pipe6
      ?$accepted
         @1
            $aa_sq[7:0] = $aa[3:0] ** 2;
            $bb_sq[7:0] = $bb[3:0] ** 2;
```
Also: `makerchip_examples/knight_rider.tlv:22-28` uses `?$step` as a clock enable around `<=` state updates; `tutorial/mini_cpu_parameterized.tlv:340-341` (`$is_reg_condition = $is_reg;` then `?$is_reg_condition`) and `warp-v/warp-v.tlv:2090` `// Condition signals must not themselves be conditioned (currently).` explain why a separate unconditioned copy of a condition signal is made; `warp-v/warp-v.tlv:2152` `// Verilog instantiation must happen outside when conditions' scope`.

### 3.5 Pipelines with multiple stages

(tutorial) `tutorial/error_pipeline_solution.tlv:5-12`, stages need not be contiguous; SandPiper inserts the flops for `$err1` from `@1` to `@3`:
```tlv
   |comp
      ?$valid
         @1
            $err1 = $bad_input | $illegal_op;
         @3
            $err2 = $err1 | $over_flow;
         @6
            $err3 = $err2 | $div_by_zero;
```
(tutorial) `tutorial/fpga_mult_example.tlv:21-47`, six stages of partial products, each stage consuming signals produced in earlier stages by name alone:
```tlv
         // 1. A.lower * B.lower (green rectangle in surf-vhdl diagram)
         @1
            $pp1[33:0] = $aa[16:0] * $bb[16:0];
         @2
            $mm1[16:0] = $pp1[16:0];

         // 2. A.lower * B.upper (lower purple rectangle in surf-vhdl diagram)
         @2
            $pp2[51:17] = $aa[16:0] * $bb[34:17];
         @3
            $mm2[52:17] = $pp2[51:17] + $pp1[33:17];
```
(tutorial) `tutorial/mini_cpu_parameterized.tlv:156-164` and `223-228`, stage numbers as macro constants so depth is a parameter:
```tlv
// Pipeline stages for each piece of logic:
m4_define(M4_PC_MUX_STAGE, 0)
m4_define(M4_FETCH_STAGE, 0)
m4_define(M4_DECODE_STAGE, 1)
m4_define(M4_REG_RD_STAGE, 1)
m4_define(M4_EXECUTE_STAGE, 2)
m4_define(M4_BRANCH_TARGET_CALC_STAGE, 3)
m4_define(M4_MEM_STAGE, 3)
m4_define(M4_REG_WR_STAGE, 3)
```
```tlv
   |fetch
      /instr
         @M4_FETCH_STAGE
            $reset = /top<>0$reset;

         @M4_FETCH_STAGE
```
(warp-v) `warp-v/warp-v.tlv:3777-3792` uses `@m5_FETCH_STAGE` and `@m5_stage_calc(@m5_NEXT_PC_STAGE<<1)`; WARP-V never writes a literal stage number in the CPU macro. The Verilog that a 3-stage pipeline becomes is in `tutorial/verilog_pyth_example.tlv:49-61` (one `always_ff` per staged signal per stage boundary).

### 3.6 Cross-stage references (`>>N`, `<<N`, `<>0`, macro-valued N)

(tutorial) `tutorial/tdm_example.tlv:30-39`, `>>0..>>3` read older values, `<<3..<>0` read newer values of the same signal:
```tlv
      // TDM the wide vector into narrow pieces.
      @0
         $flit[3:0] = >>0$valid ? >>0$packet_in[3:0] :
                      >>1$valid ? >>1$packet_in[7:4] :
                      >>2$valid ? >>2$packet_in[11:8] :
                                  >>3$packet_in[15:12];
      // Reassemble (de-multiplex) the data into its original wide form in a
      // different pipeline.
      @4  // 3 or more. Increase to add transit time.
         ?$valid
            $packet_out[15:0] = {<<3$flit, <<2$flit, <<1$flit, <>0$flit};
```
(tutorial) `tutorial/div_example.tlv:25-27` and `34-41`, `>>M4_ITER` means "previous iteration" of an interleaved computation:
```tlv
m4_define(M4_ITER, 3)  // Separation between iterations.
                       // >>M4_ITER = previous iteration
                       // <<M4_ITER = next iteration
```
```tlv
         // Carry A and B from previous iteration.
         $aa[3:0] = $in_valid ? $aa_in : >>M4_ITER$aa;
         $bb[3:0] = $in_valid ? $bb_in : >>M4_ITER$bb;
         // Valid iteration for this pipeline?
         $calc_valid = $reset ? 0 :
              $in_valid || (>>M4_ITER$calc_valid && (>>M4_ITER$iteration != 3));
